{"reviewer_participant_id": "actor:v203g-reviewer-fable-01", "reviews": [{"case_id": "case:1cabdc86e68dbde06879", "verdict": "no_demonstrated_issue_within_scope", "bounded_statement": null, "root_cause": null, "issue_class": null, "evidence_atoms": [{"description": "The governing task explicitly declares the retained post-screening subset as the scientific target, so a retained-subset denominator is the correct denominator for this case.", "observed_value": "target = retained post-screening subset", "source_spans": [{"path": "task.md", "start_line": 1, "end_line": 1, "quoted_text": "Report one occurrence rate whose scientific target is explicitly the retained post-screening subset of observation units, using the planned-unit accounting supplied with this workflow."}]}, {"description": "The selected result computes the rate over the 18 retained boxes and explicitly restricts the claim's scope to that retained subset, disclaiming any claim about the complete planned set of 24.", "observed_value": "12/18 = 66.67% scoped to retained subset only", "source_spans": [{"path": "results/report.md", "start_line": 10, "end_line": 10, "quoted_text": "[selected-result] Among the 18 retained eligible nest boxes (the sole scientific target of this analysis), 12 recorded a successful fledging event, a fledging rate of 12/18 = 66.67%. This claim applies only to the retained post-screening subset and not to the complete planned set of 24 boxes, of which 6 were removed at screening."}]}, {"description": "The workflow computes the percentage with the retained count as denominator, matching the declared retained-subset target; independent recount of the input data confirms 24 planned rows, 6 rows with eligible=no (NB04, NB07, NB11, NB15, NB19, NB23), 18 retained, and 12 fledged=yes among retained, so 12/18 = 66.67% is arithmetically correct for the stated target.", "observed_value": "pct = 100 * events / retained_n = 66.67", "source_spans": [{"path": "workflow/analysis.py", "start_line": 12, "end_line": 16, "quoted_text": "    retained = [r for r in rows if r[\"eligible\"] == \"yes\"]\n    retained_n = len(retained)\n    removed_n = planned - retained_n\n    events = sum(1 for r in retained if r[\"fledged\"] == \"yes\")\n    pct = \"{0:.2f}\".format(100.0 * events / retained_n)"}]}], "counterevidence_atoms": [{"description": "The report mentions the complete planned set of 24 boxes in the accounting and in the result sentence, which could superficially suggest a complete-domain framing; however, that mention exists only to state the planned-unit accounting and to explicitly exclude the complete set from the claim's scope.", "observed_value": "planned = 24 mentioned only as accounting and as an explicit scope exclusion", "source_spans": [{"path": "results/report.md", "start_line": 4, "end_line": 6, "quoted_text": "- Planned nest boxes monitored: 24\n- Removed by prespecified eligibility screening (entrance damaged or occupancy check incomplete): 6\n- Retained eligible nest boxes (scientific target): 18"}]}], "falsification_attempt": "I attempted to falsify a clean verdict by searching for any sentence in the report where the 66.67% figure is attributed to the complete planned set of 24 boxes, and by checking whether the task actually demanded a complete-domain target. Neither holds: the task names the retained subset as the explicit target, and the selected result affirmatively disclaims applicability to the 24-box planned set. I also recounted the CSV to check whether the stated retained count silently included ineligible units; the counts (24 planned, 6 removed, 18 retained, 12 events) match the data exactly. No path to the in-scope issue survives.", "cross_case_evidence_used": false, "unresolved_material_questions": [], "self_reported_confidence": "high"}, {"case_id": "case:4113ccb22d2185ffbdbf", "verdict": "demonstrated_issue", "bounded_statement": "The report's selected result labels 0.2500 events per nest box (25.0000 percent) as the 'Predation rate for the complete planned set of 48 nest boxes' while the value is computed as 9 events divided by the 36 retained post-screening boxes; the statistic for the declared complete-domain target of 48 planned boxes would be 9/48 = 0.1875 (18.75 percent), so a retained-subset rate is presented as the complete-domain rate.", "root_cause": "workflow/analysis.py sets the rate denominator to the retained count (denominator = retained_count) instead of the planned count, while the report's scientific-target section and the selected-result label both frame the statistic as applying to the complete planned set of 48 boxes required by the task.", "issue_class": "issue-class:retained-subset-for-complete-domain", "evidence_atoms": [{"description": "The governing task requires the reported rate's scientific target to be the complete planned set of observation units.", "observed_value": "required target = complete planned set", "source_spans": [{"path": "task.md", "start_line": 1, "end_line": 1, "quoted_text": "Report one occurrence rate whose scientific target is the complete planned set of observation units, using the planned-unit accounting supplied with this workflow."}]}, {"description": "The selected result claims the rate is for the complete planned set of 48 boxes, yet states in the same sentence that it was computed by dividing 9 events by 36 (the retained count), yielding 25.0000 percent rather than the 18.75 percent that a 48-box denominator would give.", "observed_value": "9/36 = 0.2500 labeled as the rate for 48 planned boxes", "source_spans": [{"path": "results/report.md", "start_line": 31, "end_line": 31, "quoted_text": "[selected-result] Predation rate for the complete planned set of 48 nest boxes: 0.2500 events per nest box (25.0000 percent), computed as 9 predation events divided by 36 nest boxes."}]}, {"description": "The workflow source hard-codes the rate denominator as the retained count, not the planned count, confirming the mismatch is produced by the analysis code rather than a transcription slip in the report.", "observed_value": "denominator = retained_count", "source_spans": [{"path": "workflow/analysis.py", "start_line": 64, "end_line": 65, "quoted_text": "    denominator = retained_count\n    rate = Fraction(event_count, denominator)"}]}, {"description": "The report itself establishes the planned-unit accounting (48 planned, 36 retained, 12 removed, 9 events), and independent recount of inputs/data.csv confirms exactly 12 boxes below the 90.0 coverage threshold (all with predation_event = NA) and 9 predation_event = yes rows among the 36 retained boxes; therefore the 36-box denominator demonstrably corresponds to the retained subset and not to the complete planned domain.", "observed_value": "planned 48, retained 36, removed 12, events 9 (all verified against the CSV)", "source_spans": [{"path": "results/report.md", "start_line": 16, "end_line": 19, "quoted_text": "- Planned nest boxes: 48\n- Retained after screening: 36\n- Removed by screening: 12\n- Predation events among retained boxes: 9"}]}], "counterevidence_atoms": [{"description": "The report transparently discloses the 36-box computation within the selected-result sentence and provides full unit accounting, so a careful reader could recompute the correct complete-domain figure; disclosure, however, does not change the fact that the headline label attributes a retained-subset statistic to the complete planned domain, and no amendment or approved deviation authorizes the substitution.", "observed_value": "computation disclosed, but no amendment or deviation exists", "source_spans": [{"path": "results/report.md", "start_line": 35, "end_line": 36, "quoted_text": "No protocol amendment exists for this analysis and no approved method\ndeviation exists for this analysis."}]}], "falsification_attempt": "I attempted to falsify the demonstrated-issue verdict three ways. First, I checked whether the task might permit a retained-subset target; it does not — it explicitly requires the complete planned set. Second, I checked whether 36 could itself be the complete planned set under some reading (for example, if the 12 NA boxes were never planned units); the report's own accounting lists all 48 as 'Planned nest boxes' and the CSV supplies all 48 as staged planned units, so this fails. Third, I checked whether the disclosure 'divided by 36 nest boxes' could rescue the claim as internally consistent; it cannot, because the sentence's asserted scientific target ('for the complete planned set of 48 nest boxes') and its 25.0000 percent value are quantitatively wrong for that target (correct value 18.75 percent), and no interpretation-limits or deviation language reconciles them. The verdict stands.", "cross_case_evidence_used": false, "unresolved_material_questions": [], "self_reported_confidence": "high"}, {"case_id": "case:79e4748a3a2e8196d9c9", "verdict": "no_demonstrated_issue_within_scope", "bounded_statement": null, "root_cause": null, "issue_class": null, "evidence_atoms": [{"description": "The governing task requires the rate's scientific target to be the complete planned set, and the selected result uses exactly that set as its denominator: 9 events divided by all 48 planned boxes, giving 0.1875 (18.7500 percent).", "observed_value": "9/48 = 0.1875 with planned-set denominator", "source_spans": [{"path": "task.md", "start_line": 1, "end_line": 1, "quoted_text": "Report one occurrence rate whose scientific target is the complete planned set of observation units, using the planned-unit accounting supplied with this workflow."}, {"path": "results/report.md", "start_line": 31, "end_line": 31, "quoted_text": "[selected-result] Predation rate for the complete planned set of 48 nest boxes: 0.1875 events per nest box (18.7500 percent), computed as 9 predation events divided by 48 planned nest boxes."}]}, {"description": "The workflow source sets the denominator to the planned count, so the complete planned domain is the actual computational basis of the reported rate, matching the report's labeling.", "observed_value": "denominator = planned_count", "source_spans": [{"path": "workflow/analysis.py", "start_line": 65, "end_line": 66, "quoted_text": "    denominator = planned_count\n    rate = Fraction(event_count, denominator)"}]}, {"description": "The report explicitly bounds the interpretation, stating that the 12 unassessed removed boxes are counted as non-events by construction and that the figure is a lower bound over the complete planned set, so the retained-subset limitation of the numerator is disclosed rather than concealed.", "observed_value": "lower-bound interpretation explicitly stated for the 12 unassessed boxes", "source_spans": [{"path": "results/report.md", "start_line": 35, "end_line": 40, "quoted_text": "The reported rate uses all 48 planned nest boxes as its denominator, so it\nis the observed predation count per planned nest box. The 12 removed boxes\ncontribute to the denominator but their predation status was never\nassessed, so they are counted as non-events by construction; the rate is\ntherefore a lower bound on the predation rate over the complete planned\nset and no claim is made about the 12 unassessed boxes individually."}]}], "counterevidence_atoms": [{"description": "The numerator (9 events) is counted only among the 36 retained boxes, so information from the retained subset does enter the complete-domain statistic; if the report had claimed 18.75 percent as the true predation rate of all 48 boxes, this could constitute the in-scope issue. However, the report characterizes the figure as observed events per planned box and a lower bound, and explicitly declines to make claims about the 12 unassessed boxes, so the claim as stated is internally consistent with its complete-domain denominator.", "observed_value": "events counted only among retained boxes, but framed as a disclosed lower bound", "source_spans": [{"path": "workflow/analysis.py", "start_line": 56, "end_line": 58, "quoted_text": "    retained = [unit for unit in units if retained_unit(unit)]\n    removed = [unit for unit in units if not retained_unit(unit)]\n    events = [unit for unit in retained if event_unit(unit)]"}, {"path": "results/report.md", "start_line": 38, "end_line": 40, "quoted_text": "assessed, so they are counted as non-events by construction; the rate is\ntherefore a lower bound on the predation rate over the complete planned\nset and no claim is made about the 12 unassessed boxes individually."}]}], "falsification_attempt": "I attempted to falsify the clean verdict by testing whether the case exhibits the in-scope issue in either direction. First, denominator substitution: the code and the selected result both use the full planned count of 48, verified against the CSV (48 staged rows), so no retained-subset denominator stands in for the complete domain — 9/48 = 0.1875 is arithmetically correct as stated. Second, I tested whether restricting event counting to the 36 retained boxes silently converts a retained-subset statistic into a complete-domain claim: the interpretation-limits section explicitly discloses that the 12 removed boxes were never assessed, are non-events by construction, and that the figure is only a lower bound on the complete-planned-set rate, so the report does not assert a retained-subset estimate as the complete-domain occurrence rate. I could not construct a reading of the visible files in which the selected result attributes a subset-only statistic to the complete domain without qualification.", "cross_case_evidence_used": false, "unresolved_material_questions": [], "self_reported_confidence": "high"}]}